WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.870
In one school I support, a teacher

2
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.350
bans chatbots, and the teacher next

3
00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.050
door sets homework with one.

4
00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.750
The pupils have no idea what the rule is.

5
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.790
Today: write your school’s

6
00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.430
AI policy, together.

7
00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.190
A school AI policy shouldn’t be

8
00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:20.730
a list of allowed apps.

9
00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.350
Apps change every few months, and the list

10
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.190
is out of date before it’s printed.

11
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.060
Principles last: they guide

12
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.560
decisions about any tool, new or old.

13
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.640
Start with seven principles.

14
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.260
Purpose: AI is used only

15
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.480
when it helps learning.

16
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.760
People stay responsible: a teacher

17
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.620
checks what AI makes, and AI never

18
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.300
decides alone about a pupil.

19
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.040
Privacy: no pupils’ names, marks, photos

20
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.420
or voices go into public tools.

21
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.160
Fairness: check that tools work for all

22
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.400
your pupils, in their languages.

23
00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.990
Openness: say when AI was used,

24
00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.970
staff and pupils alike.

25
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.350
Honesty in assessment: every task

26
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.170
says how much AI is allowed.

27
00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.450
And equal access: no pupil falls behind

28
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.710
for having no phone or no data.

29
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.990
So a school AI policy is a short

30
00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.090
set of shared principles, written

31
00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.410
with the people it affects.

32
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.680
Staff draft it, then ask pupils

33
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.180
and parents, at a meeting or in

34
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.200
the class WhatsApp group.

35
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.070
Agree it, put it on one page,

36
00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.010
and review it every year.

37
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.540
It’s like the rules for a

38
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.680
shared village well.

39
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.860
They work because the

40
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.180
families agreed them.

41
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.720
When the season changes, they

42
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.300
meet and change them.

43
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:50.970
Quick check!

44
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.160
Why write principles, not a

45
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.560
list of approved apps?

46
00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.760
Apps change, and principles still apply.

47
00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.490
When a new tool arrives, nobody

48
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.290
rewrites the policy: they ask if it

49
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.350
serves learning, protects privacy

50
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.570
and works for every pupil.

51
00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.550
Choosing a reading app, for example?

52
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.660
Use a weighted decision

53
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:15.920
matrix, in the open.

54
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.500
First the committee agrees the

55
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.340
criteria, like learning, privacy,

56
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.700
offline use and cost, and their weights.

57
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.240
Then score each app, multiply by

58
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.700
the weights and add up.

59
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.880
Publish the scores, so

60
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.100
everyone sees why one app won.

61
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.300
In the app, you’ll choose a class

62
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.740
trip, weighing cost, learning,

63
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.620
travel time and safety.

64
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:43.930
With the starting weights, the

65
00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.030
museum and the science centre tie.

66
00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.110
Raise learning by one point, and

67
00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.290
the science centre wins.

68
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.340
Small changes decide, so

69
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.740
agree the weights openly.

70
00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:56.560
Your turn.

71
00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.430
I scored three apps first, then picked

72
00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.470
weights that made my favourite win.

73
00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.290
Is that a fair decision?

74
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.150
No: agree the weights before scoring, or

75
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.190
the matrix just dresses up a favourite.

76
00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.310
Remember: principles, not app

77
00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:17.070
lists; written with pupils, parents

78
00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.510
and staff; reviewed every year; and

79
00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.250
tools chosen in the open.

80
00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.470
Try it in class tomorrow: a

81
00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.030
“Class AI charter”.

82
00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.750
In groups, pupils suggest rules

83
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.550
for using AI in your class.

84
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.060
Together, choose five, vote on the

85
00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.760
wording and put the charter up.

86
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:38.730
Review it at the end of term.
